xray-core-windows-pvpn1.patch - Xray-core v26.3.27 as the Windows installers ship it

Applied by scripts/libxray/build-xray-windows.sh to the upstream tag v26.3.27
(commit d2758a023cd7f4174a5a5fa4ff66e487d4342ba0). The files it changes stay
under MPL-2.0, and this patch is their source in that form. Every change
carries its own test, and the build script runs those tests before it builds.

Why (16.11.2026, a split by programs on Windows 11): in that mode a second
xray runs as the tunnel itself (TUN inbound) and routes every new flow by the
program that opened it. Stock 26.3.27 then dies under a burst of new
connections - a game launcher, a torrent client, a browser reopening its tabs -
with "fatal error: thread exhaustion", and the tunnel goes down with it.
Measured on Windows 11 ARM with 8000 UDP and 7500 TCP flows opened at once:
the stock engine reached 9885 OS threads and died; this one peaked at 34
threads and stayed up.

1. common/net/find_process_windows.go, common/net/process_table_cache.go:
   the `process` condition copied the whole system connection table for every
   new connection and opened the process again for its path. Both are
   blocking system calls, and Go gives each goroutine blocked in one its own
   OS thread. Lookups that arrive together now share one copy of a table, a
   copy answers by itself for a short while, and process paths are kept
   briefly with a bound on how many are asked for at once.
2. transport/internet/interface_index_cache.go, sockopt_windows.go: an
   outbound bound to an interface (`sockopt.interface`) listed every adapter
   (net.InterfaceByName) for each socket. The index is now asked once per
   name and kept for a second.
3. common/net/net.go: the local address list is refreshed by one caller at a
   time; the others keep the previous answer meanwhile.
4. proxy/tun/udp_fullcone.go: closing an old UDP flow could remove the newer
   flow from the same source (as upstream fixed after this tag), and packets
   racing a close could panic on a closed channel.
5. app/router/condition.go: the process lookup joined an IPv6 source and its
   port with a bare ":", which does not parse, so no IPv6 flow ever matched a
   process rule.

